# Tests Integrity

## ⛔ NO CHEATING

- ❌ NEVER modify a test to hide a failure
- ❌ NEVER modify code AND test simultaneously to bypass validation
- ❌ NEVER delete or weaken existing tests
- ❌ NEVER skip tests

## ✅ CORRECT BEHAVIOR

- ✅ If test fails → fix the **code**, not the test
- ✅ If test is obsolete → discuss with team before removing
- ✅ Include visual regression test for each UI feature

## 🔴 Both of those are checked, not just written down

A rule that only exists in a document is a rule anyone in a hurry steps over, so
two guards read the facts themselves.

- **`task devsecops:test:check:red-is-real -- @tag`** — the method rests on *the
  test failed before the code existed*, and that fact is checkable rather than
  taken on your word. The gate reads the JUnit report the test run leaves behind
  and accepts only a scenario that was **recorded** AND **failed**. A run that
  dies while loading its files exits non-zero and records nothing: a crash, not a
  proof, and it is refused. Use it between writing the test and writing the code.
- **`task devsecops:test:check:no-cheat`** — the cheapest way to make a red
  pipeline green is not to fix the code, it is to switch the test off. This gate
  reads the lines a change ADDS and refuses `@skip`/`@wip`, `.only(`/`xScenario`
  and a non-zero `tolerance:` anywhere under a test path — and `allow_failure:
  true` or a silenced linter in files the change MODIFIES, because a file that
  arrives whole turned nothing off. It runs as the `no-cheat` merge-request job, so a switched-off check
  never reaches the default branch. The only waiver is visible: a
  `No-cheat-exempt: <why>` commit trailer.

## Visual Regression

Every test that proves something observable MUST include visual regression (`assertVisualMatch` / `assertTextVisualMatch`) at tolerance **0**.

### A baseline must be REAL output — never composed

- ✅ Render the genuine artifact: run the REAL entrypoint (`task <thing>`, never the underlying tool by hand) and capture its output **verbatim**; for files/diffs show the real `cat` / `git diff`.
- ❌ NEVER hand-compose a baseline — no prose, no reformatted summary, no cherry-picking the "important" lines. If a user running the command would not see exactly that text, it is wrong.
- ✅ Mask ONLY truly volatile fields (durations, timestamps, per-run paths, the toolbox version). Prove determinism by running the assertion strict **twice** — a fresh volatile value each run, so the mask must absorb it.
- ✅ Keep the real terminal colours (force them through the pipe; do not strip ANSI to grey).

### Prove the SETUP, not just the result

An image is worth a thousand words: show the precondition you established (the file you created, the `git diff` of what you changed) AND THEN the result. A test that only captures the final state cannot prove its own setup.

**Count the baselines: a result-only scenario is a half-job.** Every scenario that screenshots a command's verdict MUST be preceded by a setup baseline (`$ cat <input-file>`, `$ git diff`) so a human reading the images understands the journey without guessing. Rule of thumb: a scenario with exactly one `assertTextVisualMatch` almost always forgot its setup.

### A discriminating proof needs BOTH halves

To prove "X is handled but Y is not", show BOTH — a positive case and a negative case (a symmetric pair). A lone "nothing happened" baseline is ambiguous (broken vs intentionally ignored). Keep the meaningful check programmatic (throw on violation); the visual is the proof, not the test.

### Whole-journey scenarios — the storyboard is the reference pattern

Any NEW journey scenario, and any REFACTOR of an existing one, MUST follow the storyboard pattern meticulously: ONE SVG assembled from real per-moment PNG frames tells the whole journey — a reviewer grasps it at a glance, and every title, note and reproduce command around the frames is selectable, copyable text (including the header's re-run command for exactly this scenario).
The whole API ships with the framework in `.config/codeceptjs/storyboard.js` (plugin + step helpers + SVG renderer) — use it as-is, never reinvent it; the engine guide is `.config/codeceptjs/README.md`.

- ✅ ONE sentence = ONE card = ONE pixel baseline: EVERY sentence of the scenario — the Given included — is registered through `storyboardStep(Given|When|Then, sentence, fn)` from `.config/codeceptjs/storyboard.js`, whose pattern string is BOTH the scenario line and the card's title, and closes on a real capture asserted INSIDE the step via `addStoryboardFrame(I, png)` (tolerance: 0)
  — a visual regression fails on the exact sentence whose image drifted, and the feature and the storyboard cannot drift apart, by construction. A sentence without a visual proof does not belong in a storyboard scenario.
- ✅ The `.feature` is the single human-authored source: it carries the sentences AND the structured comments attached to the sentence below them — `# Chapter: Title` (a chapter band), `# Note: text` (the card's explanation) and `# Copy: command` (the copyable command under the card).
  The step file only drives the app and captures the proof — cards open automatically for every sentence; JS opts or `storyboard.annotate({ note, copy })` stay ONLY for a note/copy computed at runtime.
- ✅ Tell the whole journey: BEFORE (the state on the remote system) → the acting (the live session, its ephemeral screens captured at their instant — interactive menus never coexist with the result, so snapshot each moment as it happens with `capturePageFrame(I, name)`) → AFTER (the remote proof).
  Prefer few BIG stories over many small ones, and split a long one into chapters with a `# Chapter: Title` line inside the Scenario body — the engine draws a full-width band before the next card.
- ✅ Register the `storyboard` plugin in codecept.conf.js (see codecept.conf.sample.js): it fills the SVG header from the Gherkin metadata and renders the board when the test ends — cards in reading order, two per row (the two cards of a row fully uniform: a shared image slot and a shared text zone), a band before each chapter.
  The layout is flat: `_output/<act>/<story-tag>.svg` on every outcome, and the committed copy at `storyboards/<act>/<story-tag>.svg` only on a PASSED `TASK_E2E_UPDATE_BASELINES=1` run, rebuilt from the reviewed baselines.
- ✅ Twin every visual fact with a programmatic assert of the same fact (working tree, REST on the remote branch) so a regression fails loud even without eyes.
- ❌ NEVER scatter journey baselines outside their storyboard (a frame without its card tells no story), and NEVER compose or retouch card content — every pixel inside a card is a real capture taken at its instant.

### Mechanics

1. Wait for stability (loader inactive; key element visible with `TIMEOUTS.STABLE`).
2. Capture via a Page Object: `I.saveScreenshot()` + `I.assertVisualMatch()`.

- ❌ Inline screenshot without a Page Object
- ❌ `I.saveScreenshot()` without `I.assertVisualMatch()`
- ❌ Hardcoded `I.wait(2)` (use `TIMEOUTS`)

### When a visual test fails — the only honest fixes

- Content differs → fix the **code** so the output is correct.
- Rendering differs (font / DPI / sub-pixel) → fix the deterministic launch args; do NOT raise tolerance, lower threshold, or skip the assertion.
- Legitimate evolution (e.g. a version bump) → regenerate the baseline in the SAME environment, and only after you understand exactly why it changed.
